00:13:11,570 --> 00:13:15,630
people who have atypical
HSV infections, or

241
00:13:15,630 --> 00:13:18,350
you're seeing populations
of people with varicella

242
00:13:18,350 --> 00:13:22,790
zoster or HSV, Epstein-Barr
virus, or cytomegalovirus.

243
00:13:22,790 --> 00:13:25,050
It's important there to
consider deficiencies

244
00:13:25,050 --> 00:13:27,870
in enumeration of your
natural killer cells.

245
00:13:30,150 --> 00:13:33,510
Along with natural killer
cells, it's also important

246
00:13:33,510 --> 00:13:36,790
to consider the function
and number of your T-cells,

247
00:13:36,790 --> 00:13:40,350
and deficiencies is either
the level or the function

248
00:13:40,350 --> 00:13:43,130
of T-cells will lead
to combined pictures of

249
00:13:43,150 --> 00:13:46,450
immunodeficiency. There's a few
that we've already mentioned,

250
00:13:46,450 --> 00:13:49,070
but the levels you should
be checking for are cluster

251
00:13:49,070 --> 00:13:52,830
of differentiation 2 as
well as 3, and a few that

252
00:13:52,830 --> 00:13:55,030
have been mentioned a few
times in this lecture are

253
00:13:55,030 --> 00:13:58,690
cluster of differentiation
4 and CD8, which actually

254
00:13:58,690 --> 00:14:02,190
play a role in thymic
aplasia, which is also seen in

255
00:14:02,190 --> 00:14:04,750
DeGeorge syndrome, as
well as severe combined

256
00:14:04,750 --> 00:14:09,110
immunodeficiency, and ataxia
telangiectasia, and this is

257
00:14:09,110 --> 00:14:13,210
compounded by a deficiency or
alteration in the ATM gene.

258
00:14:15,610 --> 00:14:18,610
Now we've been discussing
a lot of the confirmatory

259
00:14:18,610 --> 00:14:21,350
testing for the immune
function, but one thing I

260
00:14:21,350 --> 00:14:24,390
want to point out is here
on the top radiograph or

261
00:14:24,390 --> 00:14:27,250
chest x-ray, you'll see
somebody who has an actual

262
00:14:27,250 --> 00:14:30,150
thymic shadow, but what
we see on the bottom

263
00:14:30,150 --> 00:14:32,810
picture in a child, because
adults don't have the

264
00:14:32,810 --> 00:14:35,630
thymus, the bottom picture
actually shows somebody

265
00:14:35,630 --> 00:14:39,150
without a thymic shadow.
Now this actually points to

266
00:14:39,150 --> 00:14:42,410
various conditions. We've
mentioned DeGeorge syndrome,

267
00:14:42,410 --> 00:14:44,330
but in this case we're
dealing with somebody

268
00:14:44,330 --> 00:14:49,320
who actually has severe
combined immunodeficiency.

269
00:14:49,910 --> 00:14:52,550
This will often be referred
to, at least in the

270
00:14:52,550 --> 00:14:55,450
picture of the thymic
shadow, as a sale sign.

271
00:14:57,330 --> 00:15:00,250
While this lecture in
its entirety is not

272
00:15:00,250 --> 00:15:03,170
about HIV, I think it's
important to consider,

273
00:15:03,170 --> 00:15:04,950
especially since we've
been talking about

274
00:15:04,950 --> 00:15:08,650
cluster differentiation
4 cells or CD4 cells,

275
00:15:08,650 --> 00:15:12,010
some of the screening
available for HIV. Here we're

276
00:15:12,010 --> 00:15:16,110
doing an ELISA assay, also
considering the surface antigen

277
00:15:16,110 --> 00:15:19,770
as well as IgM and IgG
with confirmatory testing

278
00:15:19,770 --> 00:15:23,450
using nucleic acid amplification.
Furthermore, there's

279
00:15:23,450 --> 00:15:28,290
also PCR testing or quantitative
PCR of the viral RNA and

280
00:15:28,290 --> 00:15:30,770
all of the treatments that
we're considering are not

281
00:15:30,770 --> 00:15:33,950
looking at just the infections
but ways to measure and

282
00:15:33,950 --> 00:15:37,050
increase the immune response
and increase CD4 count.

283
00:15:39,210 --> 00:15:42,690
In regards to further testing
of lymphocyte function

284
00:15:42,690 --> 00:15:45,670
or T cells, one thing we
can check is stimulation

285
00:15:45,670 --> 00:15:49,450
of T cells. And we do this by
using the phytohemagglutinin

286
00:15:50,180 --> 00:15:52,830
assay. And what this will
check for is actually

287
00:15:52,830 --> 00:15:55,230
your multiplication of
T cells as well as the

288
00:15:55,230 --> 00:15:59,300
amounts. So what we do is we
mix both the phytohemagglutinin

289
00:15:59,300 --> 00:16:02,770
and T cells. And if we
see a low stimulatory

290
00:16:02,770 --> 00:16:05,730
index, this actually points
to decreased function.

291
00:16:05,730 --> 00:16:08,230
And what you'll see is
a sequelae of this is

292
00:16:08,230 --> 00:16:11,130
actually chronic
mucocutaneous disease. and in

293
00:16:11,130 --> 00:16:14,130
some cases over-proliferated
candida infections.

294
00:16:14,290 --> 00:16:17,530
In order to test for
cytokine production, one

295
00:16:17,530 --> 00:16:20,450
thing we can check is an
interferon gamma release

296
00:16:20,450 --> 00:16:23,050
assay, and what this
does, especially in the

297
00:16:23,050 --> 00:16:26,170
setting of interleucan
or IL-12 receptor

298
00:16:26,170 --> 00:16:28,450
deficiencies, is you'll
find that there's actually

299
00:16:28,450 --> 00:16:31,610
decreased levels of
interferon gamma release.

300
00:16:31,910 --> 00:16:34,430
In the setting of
severe combined

301
00:16:34,630 --> 00:16:37,730
immunodeficiency, one thing
we can check or use is

302
00:16:37,730 --> 00:16:41,130
a T-cell receptor excision
assay. And what this

303
00:16:41,130 --> 00:16:44,450
checks for is actually the
circular DNA in T-cells,

304
00:16:44,450 --> 00:16:47,420
and it's actually a measure
of sufficient or adequate

305
00:16:47,420 --> 00:16:50,350
thymic output. So in
this case, if you have a

306
00:16:50,350 --> 00:16:53,690
normal T-cell receptor
excision assay, you have

307
00:16:53,690 --> 00:16:57,130
normal T-cell production
and use and functionality.

308
00:16:57,130 --> 00:17:00,290
However, decreases will
tell you a little bit more

309
00:17:00,290 --> 00:17:02,930
or point you more towards
decreased thymic output.

310
00:17:04,530 --> 00:17:08,070
Moving forward from natural
killer cells and B cells,

311
00:17:08,070 --> 00:17:10,950
one thing we can also look
at is the function and

312
00:17:10,950 --> 00:17:14,330
quantity of B cells. Here,
we're going to be checking

313
00:17:14,330 --> 00:17:18,830
for levels of CD19 as well
as CD20, and I've included

314
00:17:18,830 --> 00:17:22,730
a few of the pertinent
disease processes below. We've

315
00:17:22,730 --> 00:17:27,250
already talked about SCID
and ataxia telangiectasia,

316
00:17:27,250 --> 00:17:30,370
but two other conditions
are X-linked Bruton's A

317
00:17:30,370 --> 00:17:34,050
-gamma globulinemia, which
actually has a male predominance.

318
00:17:34,050 --> 00:17:36,170
So if you're considering
somebody and you've

319
00:17:36,170 --> 00:17:39,390
gotten the history of a male
with recurrent infections,

320
00:17:39,390 --> 00:17:42,970
this does put X-linked
Bruton's A-gamma globulinemia

321
00:17:42,970 --> 00:17:45,890
into your differential
versus your female patients.

322
00:17:46,290 --> 00:17:47,270
Furthermore,

323
00:17:47,450 --> 00:17:49,850
another disease state
with increased B cells

324
00:17:49,850 --> 00:17:53,230
is actually pre-B acute
lymphoblastic leukemia.

325
00:17:56,890 --> 00:17:59,790
Outside of what we've already
mentioned in regards to

326
00:17:59,790 --> 00:18:03,050
T cells, B cells, natural
killer cells, there's also

327
00:18:03,050 --> 00:18:06,130
deficiencies and abnormalities
in certain messenger

328
00:18:06,130 --> 00:18:09,650
proteins which can lead to
several disease states. Many

329
00:18:09,650 --> 00:18:12,770
of these are commonly seen
in the clinical setting as

330
00:18:12,770 --> 00:18:15,670
well as tested heavily on
the boards and I've included

331
00:18:15,670 --> 00:18:18,690
a few additional clinical
scenarios along with these

332
00:18:18,690 --> 00:18:22,070
slides. So the abnormalities
that you'll see here are

333
00:18:22,070 --> 00:18:25,930
in complement protein,
interleukins, or interferons.

334
00:18:27,770 --> 00:18:31,190
In checking for complement
quantity defects and

335
00:18:31,190 --> 00:18:34,330
pathway defects, we can
use two tests such as the

336
00:18:34,330 --> 00:18:39,170
CH50 and AH50. So with
CH50, this is testing the

337
00:18:39,170 --> 00:18:42,450
classical pathway, and
it's a hemolytic assay. So

338
00:18:42,450 --> 00:18:44,590
what that means is we're
taking complement from

339
00:18:44,590 --> 00:18:47,310
your donor or your patient,
and we're seeing the

340
00:18:47,310 --> 00:18:50,430
ability from this donor
complement to lice sheep's

341
00:18:50,430 --> 00:18:53,850
blood. And so this is
actually a nonspecific test.

342
00:18:53,990 --> 00:18:57,370
The AH50 is the alternative
pathway that's being

343
00:18:57,370 --> 00:18:59,610
tested and it's also
a hemolytic complement

344
00:18:59,610 --> 00:19:02,170
assay. You'll specifically
see this being

345
00:19:02,170 --> 00:19:04,510
referenced in regards to
Neisserial infections,

346
00:19:04,510 --> 00:19:07,050
which is the most common
indication, and they

347
00:19:07,050 --> 00:19:11,590
determine deficiencies in
C3 as well as C5 to C9.

348
00:19:11,970 --> 00:19:15,010
Furthermore, you can test
for the actual number or

349
00:19:15,010 --> 00:19:18,890
enumeration of C1 to C9,
and in some diseases like

350
00:19:18,890 --> 00:19:23,030
systemic lupus, you'll see
deficiencies in C1 through C4,

351
00:19:23,030 --> 00:19:26,470
and in Neisseria bacteremia
or Neisseria infections,

352
00:19:26,470 --> 00:19:30,150
you'll see deficiencies
in C5 through C9 pathways.

353
00:19:34,150 --> 00:19:37,230
This is actually something
that I've definitely seen

354
00:19:37,230 --> 00:19:40,110
in test questions and also
seen in clinical practice,

355
00:19:40,110 --> 00:19:43,490
and it's the complement
deficiency of C1 esterase

356
00:19:43,490 --> 00:19:46,150
inhibitor, and what this
can put you at higher risk

357
00:19:46,150 --> 00:19:50,110
for is hereditary angioedema.
The other reason I put

358
00:19:50,110 --> 00:19:52,730
this into this slide is
because it really ties in

359
00:19:52,730 --> 00:19:55,690
not only the deficiency
in the complement but good

360
00:19:55,690 --> 00:19:58,730
history and physical
examination. Obviously it's hard

361
00:19:58,730 --> 00:20:01,830
to miss the angioedema
here but it's important to

362
00:20:01,830 --> 00:20:04,910
know if patients are taking
medications like ACE inhibitors

363
00:20:04,910 --> 00:20:07,530
putting them at higher
risk for angioedema as

364
00:20:07,530 --> 00:20:10,470
well as familial predispositions
and family history.

365
00:20:12,770 --> 00:20:16,430
In some of the disorders so
far in regards to complement,

366
00:20:16,430 --> 00:20:18,790
we've talked about
different deficiencies as

367
00:20:18,790 --> 00:20:21,790
well as issues in the pathway
and that's some of the

368
00:20:21,790 --> 00:20:24,790
testing that we had discussed
on the prior slides. The

369
00:20:24,790 --> 00:20:27,190
other thing to mention with
complement disorders is

370
00:20:27,190 --> 00:20:30,090
that what you'll see when
complement is involved is

371
00:20:30,090 --> 00:20:32,910
that sometimes levels will
actually be lower because

372
00:20:32,910 --> 00:20:35,610
they're being consumed and
then they'll also be lower

373
00:20:35,610 --> 00:20:37,960
in the cases where there's
going to be deposition.

374
00:20:38,010 --> 00:20:41,650
So in the case of lupus
nephritis, you'll actually see

375
00:20:41,650 --> 00:20:45,730
consumption of the C3 and
C4. So if you're checking

376
00:20:45,730 --> 00:20:48,330
for levels, they actually
might appear to be lower.

377
00:20:48,690 --> 00:20:51,730
Furthermore, in some
conditions, you'll actually have

378
00:20:51,730 --> 00:20:55,570
antigen and antibody complexes.
And so what happens in

379
00:20:55,570 --> 00:20:58,310
this case is that these
will appear to be low when

380
00:20:58,310 --> 00:21:01,050
you're checking serum values.
However, it's because of

381
00:21:01,050 --> 00:21:04,310
the deposition that's
actually occurring. So in post

382
00:21:04,310 --> 00:21:07,190
-streptococcal glomerulonephritis,
you'll actually have low

383
00:21:07,190 --> 00:21:11,710
C3 levels because of the
antigen and antibody complex

384
00:21:11,710 --> 00:21:15,310
deposition. And it's the
same thing with MPGN, which

385
00:21:15,310 --> 00:21:18,290
is membranoproliferative
glomerulonephritis, where

386
00:21:18,290 --> 00:21:22,050
you'll have a low level of
C3 because of the deposition.

387
00:21:24,990 --> 00:21:28,470
Now along with complement,
you'll also see considerations

388
00:21:28,470 --> 00:21:30,770
of interleukins playing
roles in various

389
00:21:30,770 --> 00:21:34,010
diseases and these aren't
always specific so if you'll

390
00:21:34,010 --> 00:21:38,310
see interleukin 1a and 1b
is common in IBD but it's

391
00:21:38,310 --> 00:21:43,310
also found with IL-6 and
IL-12 and IBD. IL-5 you'll

392
00:21:43,310 --> 00:21:46,590
see is pertinent with
allergic asthma and IL-7 and

393
00:21:46,590 --> 00:21:49,530
IL-9 have been found to be
in autoimmune conditions.

394
00:21:49,550 --> 00:21:53,350
IL-6 and IL-12 you'll also
see in reference to Castleman

395
00:21:53,350 --> 00:21:55,970
disease. On the next slide
we'll talk little bit

396
00:21:55,970 --> 00:21:59,610
about the prevalence and
the new news around IL-6.

397
00:22:02,110 --> 00:22:05,290
In recent news as well as
clinical practice in the

398
00:22:05,290 --> 00:22:08,070
setting of the COVID-19
pandemic, you'll hear a

399
00:22:08,070 --> 00:22:11,070
lot about the medication
tocilizumab, which is commonly

400
00:22:11,070 --> 00:22:13,950
known by the trade named
Ectemra. What this is

401
00:22:13,950 --> 00:22:17,170
is a monoclonal antibody
and it's actually an IL

402
00:22:17,170 --> 00:22:20,890
-6 receptor inhibitor. So
this was already approved

403
00:22:20,890 --> 00:22:23,570
and being used in certain
inflammatory and autoimmune

404
00:22:23,570 --> 00:22:25,930
conditions such as different
types of arthritis,

405
00:22:25,930 --> 00:22:28,610
but we actually now see
it being used in COVID

406
00:22:28,610 --> 00:22:32,690
-19 because this modulation
of IL-6 actually plays

407
00:22:32,690 --> 00:22:35,670
a role in altering the
cytokine release pathways.

408
00:22:38,300 --> 00:22:41,610
When considering interferons,
one thing we can look at

409
00:22:41,610 --> 00:22:44,450
in terms of testing is an
interferon gamma release

410
00:22:44,450 --> 00:22:48,090
assay, and one of the biggest
examples is its use in

411
00:22:48,090 --> 00:22:51,690
determining TB infections. So
you'll hear about interferon

412
00:22:51,690 --> 00:22:54,950
gamma release assays or
quantiferon interchangeably

413
00:22:54,950 --> 00:22:58,510
in regards to tuberculosis
infection. So what happens

414
00:22:58,510 --> 00:23:01,210
here is we actually take
our white blood cells which

415
00:23:01,210 --> 00:23:04,130
will release the interferon
gamma and we mix this with

416
00:23:04,130 --> 00:23:07,870
a tuberculosis or tuberculin
antigen and we quantify

417
00:23:07,870 --> 00:23:11,750
and qualify the amount of
interferon gamma release and

418
00:23:11,750 --> 00:23:14,290
so that response actually
tells us if there's a latent

419
00:23:14,290 --> 00:23:17,110
or active infection and it's
a measure of not only the

420
00:23:17,110 --> 00:23:20,270
quality but also the quantity
or amount of response.

421
00:23:23,830 --> 00:23:27,150
Now, after talking a little
bit about interferon,

422
00:23:27,150 --> 00:23:30,070
we'll shift our attention
to antibody defects, and

423
00:23:30,070 --> 00:23:32,690
what we'll see is not only
defects and deficiencies,

424
00:23:32,730 --> 00:23:35,430
but also overproduction,
which can lead to certain

425
00:23:35,430 --> 00:23:39,490
issues as well. When we're
dealing with deficiencies,

426
00:23:39,490 --> 00:23:42,110
you'll actually see these
patients are more prone

427
00:23:42,110 --> 00:23:45,090
to sinus and pulmonary
infections, and this will

428
00:23:45,090 --> 00:23:48,410
actually give way to infections
by certain pathogens, so

429
00:23:48,410 --> 00:23:50,410
you see a higher
prevalence of streptococcus

430
00:23:50,410 --> 00:23:53,990
pneumoniae, as well as
haemophilus influenza infections.

431
00:23:54,410 --> 00:23:57,170
Furthermore, with
overproduction of antibodies

432
00:23:57,170 --> 00:23:59,390
in these defects,
you'll see things like

433
00:23:59,390 --> 00:24:01,530
multiple myeloma,
as well as a higher

434
00:24:01,530 --> 00:24:04,350
prevalence of Waldenstrand's
macroglobulinemia.

435
00:24:06,030 --> 00:24:08,730
Furthermore, you'll also
see an increased prevalence

436
00:24:08,730 --> 00:24:11,790
in that spectrum of multiple
myeloma of MGUS, which

437
00:24:11,790 --> 00:24:14,630
is monoglomal gammopathy
of unknown significance.

438
00:24:16,830 --> 00:24:20,330
Outside of looking at the
specific antibodies, we

439
00:24:20,330 --> 00:24:23,410
can also do a quantitative
evaluation of different

440
00:24:23,410 --> 00:24:27,690
immunoglobulins. First
mentioned is IgG, which is

441
00:24:27,690 --> 00:24:30,890
actually produced in response
to a specific antigen,

442
00:24:30,890 --> 00:24:33,810
and this is part of the
function that B cells

443
00:24:33,810 --> 00:24:37,250
provide. Now one thing to
keep in mind is that this is

444
00:24:37,250 --> 00:24:39,750
actually not going to
provide you with reliable

445
00:24:39,750 --> 00:24:43,270
results if your patient that
you're testing has received

446
00:24:43,270 --> 00:24:47,690
some form of IgG in the
past six months. So in

447
00:24:47,690 --> 00:24:50,550
the setting of the COVID
-19 pandemic, we're seeing

448
00:24:50,550 --> 00:24:52,610
this a lot in the
patients who have received

449
00:24:52,610 --> 00:24:56,370
monoclonal antibody therapy
within the past few months.

450
00:24:56,910 --> 00:25:01,090
Moving to IgA, you'll see
IgA deficiencies as an

451
00:25:01,090 --> 00:25:04,390
extremely common cause
of immune deficiency, and

452
00:25:04,390 --> 00:25:07,710
because IgA primarily plays
a role in your mucosal

453
00:25:07,710 --> 00:25:10,610
response, you'll see a
breakdown of this initial

454
00:25:10,610 --> 00:25:14,070
barrier leading to recurrent
mucosal infections.

455
00:25:14,270 --> 00:25:17,090
As we mentioned on
the previous slides,

456
00:25:17,210 --> 00:25:20,430
IgM has an association
and plays a role in

457
00:25:20,430 --> 00:25:24,410
Bruton's agammaglobulinemia,
and other conditions

458
00:25:24,410 --> 00:25:28,230
such as hyper-IgE or Jobe
syndrome are associated

459
00:25:28,230 --> 00:25:31,410
with, as I mentioned,
IgE, and this is actually

460
00:25:31,410 --> 00:25:34,370
a form of a type 1
hypersensitivity reaction,

461
00:25:34,370 --> 00:25:37,750
an example of type 1
hypersensitivity reaction.

462
00:25:40,150 --> 00:25:44,110
On this slide, you don't
necessarily see the workup

463
00:25:44,110 --> 00:25:47,630
and evaluation of every
specific disease, but what I

464
00:25:47,630 --> 00:25:50,110
wanted to point to is the
general trends that you

465
00:25:50,110 --> 00:25:53,650
see throughout different
disease processes. So when

466
00:25:53,650 --> 00:25:56,510
we're dealing with diseases
such as AIDS, you'll actually

467
00:25:56,510 --> 00:25:59,710
see an increase in all
immunoglobulin classes,

468
00:25:59,710 --> 00:26:03,490
and I mentioned before ataxia
telangiectasia provides

469
00:26:03,490 --> 00:26:08,630
you with a decrease in IgA,
IgE, as well as IgG. One

470
00:26:08,630 --> 00:26:10,950
of the more recent findings
that you'll be tested

471
00:26:10,950 --> 00:26:13,910
on and seen is celiac disease
actually presents with

472
00:26:13,910 --> 00:26:17,750
a low IgA, pointing to a
